/*****************************************************************************

  strange_primes.magma

  Magma code for the determination of the strange primes p < 10^5
  (Proposition 5.6 and Table 2) in

    Maarten Derickx and Michael Stoll,
    Prime order torsion on elliptic curves over number fields,
    Part I: Asymptotics.

  Written for Magma V2.29 by Claude Code (Fable 5.1), based on two
  independent versions by the authors, with improvements regarding
  speed and memory usage.

  ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
  Terminology (Definition 5.1 of the paper)
  ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

  Let p be a prime and chi a nontrivial even Dirichlet character mod p.
  A newform f in S_2(Gamma_1(p), chi) is strange if L(f, 1) = 0;
  chi is strange if there is a strange newform with character chi;
  p is strange if some chi is strange. The strangeness dimension strdim(p)
  is the number of strange newforms of level p. Strangeness is constant
  on Galois orbits of newforms (Proposition 5.3), so the set of strange characters
  is closed under Galois conjugation, and it suffices to test one character
  for each order n > 1 dividing (p-1)/2.

  ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
  Method
  ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

  Let M = M_2(Gamma_1(p), chi)^+ be the plus part of the space of modular symbols
  with character chi, let S be its cuspidal subspace, let T be the Hecke algebra,
  and let e in M be the winding element (the class of -{0, oo}).
  By Lemma 5.2, a newform f is strange if and only if e_f e = 0,
  where e_f is the idempotent in T (x) Qbar projecting onto the f-part.
  The code uses {0, oo} instead, which is Magma's WindingElement(M),
  but the sign is irrelevant for the computation.

  For a prime l != p, the operator

      P_l = (T_l - l chi(l) - 1)(T_l - chi(l) - l)

  kills the two Eisenstein eigenvectors in M and is invertible on S:
  an eigenvalue a_l(f) in {l chi(l) + 1, chi(l) + l} would by Ribet's relation
  a_l(f) = chi(l) conj(a_l(f)) force chi(l) = 1 and a_l(f) = l + 1,
  which contradicts |a_l(f)| <= 2 sqrt(l). Hence e' := e P_l is
  the cuspidal projection of e up to an invertible Hecke operator,
  and the T-module generated by e' is the sum of the f-parts of S
  over the non-strange newforms f. Consequently

      chi is strange  <==>  T e' is a proper subspace of S.

  1. Screening modulo q (screen_character, screen_prime, screen_range).

  We work over F_q for a prime q = 1 mod (p-1), so that all characters mod p
  take values in F_q and all linear algebra takes place over a small prime field.
  Everything (the modular symbols space, the Hecke operators, e')
  has a natural integral structure, and reduction mod q can only decrease
  the rank of an integral matrix. Therefore, if the F_q[T_2]-span of e'
  (a Krylov space, computed by sparse matrix-vector products) is all of S mod q,
  then the T-module generated by e' is all of S in characteristic zero,
  and chi is not strange. This certificate needs neither
  a characteristic polynomial nor a semisimplicity assumption.

  If the F_q[T_2]-span W of e' is smaller than S, this may still be
  because T_2 alone does not separate all Hecke-simple constituents of S.
  (For instance, for quadratic chi, f and its complex conjugate f (x) chi
  have the same eigenvalue under T_l whenever chi(l) = 1 or a_l(f) = 0,
  and a_l(f) = 0 is not rare for newforms with a small coefficient field.)
  We therefore test, for all primes l <= L_MAX, whether W is stable
  under T_l (with a random vector of W; this detects instability
  with probability 1 - 1/q), and whenever it is not, we enlarge W
  to the module generated by e' under T_2 and the T_l found so far.
  If W is still a proper subspace of S in the end, then chi is a candidate
  with defect d = dim S - dim W. By the rank argument above,
  d is an upper bound for the dimension over Q(chi) of the strange part of S.

  For a candidate we further determine a prime l' such that W is stable
  under T_l' (by construction, this holds for the operators used to
  generate W) and the characteristic polynomial kerpol in F_q[x]
  (of degree d) of T_l' on the quotient S/W is such that kerpol(T_l')
  is injective on W. Barring a coincidence modulo q, the roots of kerpol
  are the reductions of the eigenvalues a_l'(f) of the strange newforms,
  and in characteristic zero the kernel of kerpol(T_l') is precisely
  the strange part of S.

  2. Verification in characteristic zero (verify_candidate).

  For a candidate (p, chi) we compute M over K = Q(zeta_n), n = ord(chi),
  lift kerpol to K[x] (the coefficients are elementary symmetric functions
  of the a_l'(f); they are small algebraic integers and are recovered from
  their reductions mod q by a closest vector computation),
  let N be the kernel of kerpol(T_l') on M (this is contained in S,
  since the Eisenstein eigenvalues are not roots of kerpol), check that dim N = d,
  decompose N into Hecke-simple pieces and test for each piece
  whether the projection of the winding element onto it vanishes
  (Magma's WindingElementProjection).
  The pieces with vanishing projection are strange newform spaces.
  If their dimensions add up to d, the strange part of S is completely determined,
  since d bounds its dimension from above; the Galois orbit over Q of a newform
  in a piece A has size dim_K(A) * phi(n), and strdim(p) is the sum
  of these sizes over all strange pieces and all orders n.
  If the dimensions do not add up to d, then either the deficiency found modulo q
  was spurious (the module W was not stable under some T_l with l > L_MAX)
  or the auxiliary prime q was unlucky; neither has ever happened.
  The code says so, and rerunning with a larger L_MAX or a different Q_MIN
  resolves the situation.
  All statements printed by the code are rigorously established.

  ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
  Efficiency
  ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

  Hecke operators on modular symbols are sparse (about 130 nonzero entries
  per row for p near 10^5, where dim S is about 8000), so the Krylov sequence
  e', e' T_2, e' T_2^2, ... is computed with sparse matrix-vector products;
  the only dense operation in the common case (chi not strange, which is
  the case for all but 75 of the 115,080 pairs (p, chi) with p < 10^5,
  chi of order n > 1, one chi per n) is one rank computation.
  Screening one character takes about 30 seconds for p near 10^5 (dim S about 8000)
  on one core of a 2023 desktop machine and needs about 5 GB of memory;
  the number of characters per prime, the number of divisors of (p-1)/2 minus one,
  is 12 on average and up to 95. The auxiliary prime q is taken between 2^22
  and 2^23.5, the range in which Magma's linear algebra over F_q is fastest;
  the probability of an unlucky q is negligible.
  Screening all primes below 10^5 takes about 20 CPU days;
  this is best done in several parallel Magma processes (see check_table2 below);
  memory bandwidth is shared, so the processes slow each other down noticeably.
  The verification step for the 75 candidates takes a few CPU hours in total
  (5 minutes for p = 96289).

  Lean variant (screen_character_lean, screen_prime_lean, strange_orders_lean;
  added after the run for Table 2, for the search beyond 10^5). Instead of
  the Krylov space and its rank, it computes the linear complexity of the
  scalar sequence u . (e' T^i) for a random vector u (Wiedemann's idea,
  Berlekamp-Massey): this is a lower bound for dim F_q[T] e', so the value
  dim S certifies that the Hecke module of e' is all of S. It tries T = T_2
  and then random linear combinations of T_2, T_3, ... (up to four trials)
  and hands the remaining cases to screen_character. (The quadratic character
  with chi(2) = 1 needs a variant with two generating vectors, as every
  eigenvalue of T_2 then occurs twice; see the code.) The cuspidal subspace is
  not constructed (dim S = dim M - 2, see the code), the dense Hecke matrices
  are discarded once converted, and no dense dS x dim M matrix is ever formed.
  Results are identical; at p near 10^5 one character takes the same time
  and 3.2 instead of 5 GB, at p near 2 * 10^5 it takes 140 instead of 550
  seconds and 10 instead of 17 GB (the dense rank computation dominates the
  original code there). A long run should use one Magma process per prime
  (a Magma process slows down considerably over days); see search_prime.m
  and run_search.sh.

  ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
  Usage (B is the upper bound for the range of primes)
  ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

    load "strange_primes.magma";
    SetVerbose("User1", 1);    // progress output
    SetVerbose("User2", 1);    // detailed timings

    strange_orders(61);        // [ <6, 2, [ 2 ], true> ]: the character of order 6
                               //   is strange, strdim(61) = 2, one Galois orbit
                               //   of size 2, verification succeeded
    strange_orders(8713);      // [ <4, 4, [ 4 ], true>, <12, 4, [ 4 ], true> ]
    strange_orders(1009);      // [ ]: not strange

    cands := screen_range(2, B);    // screening only; candidates as tuples
    [verify_candidate(c) : c in cands];

    check_table2(2, B);        // compare with Table 2 for all primes in range
    check_table2_primes();     // check the 74 primes in Table 2

    // Long runs: split into parts, one Magma process each, with results files;
    // a process that was interrupted can be restarted with the same command
    // and continues where it stopped.
    // 16 is the number of processes, 3 is the index (1-based) within this number.
    check_table2(2, B : part := <3, 16>, file := "results_3.txt");
    check_results(["results_" cat IntegerToString(k) cat ".txt" : k in [1..16]]);

    strange_orders_lean(8713); // as strange_orders, with the lean screening

  ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
  Searching beyond 10^5 with GNU parallel
  ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

  The files search_prime.m, search_job.sh and run_search.sh (in the same
  directory as this file) run the lean screening and the verification with
  one Magma process per prime, queued by GNU parallel
  (https://www.gnu.org/software/parallel/):

    ./run_search.sh LO HI NJ MEMFREE     e.g.  ./run_search.sh 100001 200000 12 30G

  treats all primes LO <= p <= HI, at most NJ processes at a time, starting
  a new one only while at least MEMFREE memory is free (GNU parallel's
  --memfree; it also kills and requeues the youngest job when the free
  memory drops below half of MEMFREE). Set MAGMA=/path/to/magma in the
  environment if magma is not on the PATH. Each job runs
  "magma -b p:=<p> search_prime.m" through search_job.sh, which exits with
  status 0 only if Magma printed the final success line. The output of the
  job for p is in res/1/<p>/stdout and contains one line

    RESULT <p, candidates, verified>

  with candidates as returned by screen_prime_lean (tuples
  <p, n, q, zeta_n mod q, d, l', coefficients of kerpol>) and verified as
  returned by verify_candidate (tuples <n, strdim contribution, orbit sizes,
  confirmed>); "RESULT <p, [], []>" says that p is not strange. Collect the
  strange primes with

    grep -rh '^RESULT' res/ | grep -v '\[\], \[\]' | sort -t'<' -k2 -n

  The run is restartable: after an interruption, rerun the same command;
  finished primes are skipped and failed ones are retried (search.joblog
  has one line per finished job with its run time and exit status).

  Cost: one process needs about 6 GB of memory at p = 1.5 * 10^5 and 10 GB
  at p = 2 * 10^5 (growing like p^2, with spikes of a few seconds to about
  1.6 times that), and one prime takes on average 10 minutes at p = 10^5
  and 30 minutes at p = 1.6 * 10^5 (growing like p^2.7; individual primes
  scatter by a factor of 3 and more, mainly with the number of divisors of
  (p-1)/2). The range 10^5 < p < 2 * 10^5 takes about two weeks with 12
  processes on a desktop machine with 8 fast and 8 slow cores and 125 GB of
  memory; the processes compete for memory bandwidth, so more processes per
  core give diminishing returns. On a larger machine, choose NJ from the
  memory available per process at the upper end of the range, and MEMFREE
  at about three times the memory of one process.

*****************************************************************************/

// The auxiliary prime q is the smallest prime q > Q_MIN with q = 1 mod (p-1).
// Keep Q_MIN below 2^23 so that q < 2^23.5 (fast linear algebra over F_q).
Q_MIN := 2^22;

// In case of a rank deficiency, the Hecke module of e' is tested
// for stability under T_l for all primes l <= L_MAX (see above).
L_MAX := 30;

// Table 2 of the paper: <p, order of chi, strdim>.
TABLE2 := [
  <61, 6, 2>, <97, 12, 4>, <101, 10, 4>, <181, 6, 2>, <193, 12, 4>, <409, 12, 4>,
  <421, 6, 2>, <733, 6, 2>, <853, 6, 2>, <1021, 30, 8>, <1777, 3, 2>, <1801, 5, 4>,
  <1861, 6, 6>, <2377, 12, 4>, <2917, 6, 2>, <3229, 3, 2>, <3793, 12, 4>, <4201, 3, 2>,
  <4733, 7, 6>, <5441, 10, 4>, <5821, 6, 2>, <5953, 3, 2>, <6133, 6, 2>, <6781, 6, 2>,
  <7477, 14, 6>, <8681, 10, 4>, <8713, 4, 4>, <8713, 12, 4>, <10093, 6, 2>, <11497, 3, 2>,
  <12941, 10, 4>, <14533, 6, 2>, <15061, 6, 4>, <15289, 12, 4>, <17041, 3, 2>, <17053, 6, 2>,
  <17257, 12, 4>, <18199, 3, 4>, <20341, 6, 2>, <22093, 6, 2>, <23017, 12, 4>, <23593, 12, 4>,
  <26161, 3, 2>, <26177, 4, 4>, <28201, 3, 2>, <29569, 2, 2>, <31033, 3, 2>, <31657, 3, 2>,
  <32497, 3, 2>, <35521, 3, 2>, <35537, 4, 4>, <36373, 6, 2>, <39313, 12, 4>, <41081, 5, 4>,
  <41131, 3, 2>, <41593, 12, 4>, <42793, 3, 2>, <48733, 6, 2>, <52561, 3, 2>, <52691, 5, 4>,
  <53113, 12, 4>, <53857, 12, 4>, <63313, 12, 4>, <63901, 6, 2>, <65171, 5, 4>, <65449, 12, 4>,
  <66973, 6, 2>, <68737, 12, 4>, <69061, 6, 2>, <69401, 5, 4>, <69457, 4, 4>, <73009, 12, 4>,
  <86113, 12, 4>, <86161, 4, 4>, <96289, 12, 4> ];

//============================================================================
// Auxiliary functions
//============================================================================

// The auxiliary prime for the level p.
function aux_prime(p)
  m := p - 1;
  q := (Q_MIN div m)*m + 1;
  repeat q +:= m; until IsPrime(q);
  return q;
end function;

// vec * pol(mat), computed by Horner's scheme with deg(pol)
// matrix-vector products (mat may be a sparse matrix).
function pol_mat_vec(pol, mat, vec)
  if IsZero(pol) then return Parent(vec)!0; end if;
  result := LeadingCoefficient(pol)*vec;
  for i := Degree(pol)-1 to 0 by -1 do
    result := result*mat + Coefficient(pol, i)*vec;
  end for;
  return result;
end function;

// The Hecke operator T_l on the modular symbols space M as a sparse matrix.
function sparse_hecke(M, l)
  return SparseMatrix(HeckeOperator(M, l));
end function;

// e * (T_l - l chi(l) - 1)(T_l - chi(l) - l), where T is the matrix of T_l
// and cl = chi(l).
function eisenstein_killer(e, T, l, cl)
  v := e*T - (l*cl + 1)*e;
  return v*T - (cl + l)*v;
end function;

// The Krylov sequence e, e T, e T^2, ..., e T^(k-1).
function krylov_rows(e, T, k)
  rows := [e];
  v := e;
  for i := 2 to k do
    v := v*T;
    Append(~rows, v);
  end for;
  return rows;
end function;

// The smallest subspace of VS containing W that is stable
// under the (sparse) matrices ops[l], l in gens; the computation stops
// as soon as the dimension dS of the cuspidal subspace is reached.
function saturate(W, VS, ops, gens, dS)
  repeat
    dimW := Dimension(W);
    for l in gens do
      B := Basis(W);
      W := sub<VS | B cat [VS!(b*ops[l]) : b in B]>;
      if Dimension(W) eq dS then return W; end if;
    end for;
  until Dimension(W) eq dimW;
  return W;
end function;

//============================================================================
// Step 1: screening modulo q
//============================================================================

// chi: a nontrivial even Dirichlet character of prime modulus p
//      with values in F_q, where q = 1 mod (p-1) is a prime.
// Returns a tuple <d, l, kerpol, z>, where
//   d = 0 if chi is certified to be not strange (then l = 2, kerpol = 1);
//   otherwise d >= 1 is an upper bound for the dimension over Q(chi)
//   of the strange part of S_2(Gamma_1(p), chi), l is a prime
//   and kerpol is a monic polynomial of degree d over F_q,
//   as described at the top of this file (input for verify_candidate);
//   z = chi(g) in F_q for g = PrimitiveRoot(p) identifies chi.
function screen_character(chi)
  t0 := Cputime();
  p := Modulus(chi);
  n := Order(chi);
  Fq := BaseRing(chi);
  PF := PolynomialRing(Fq);
  z := chi(PrimitiveRoot(p));
  M := ModularSymbols(chi, 2, +1);
  S := CuspidalSubspace(M);
  dS := Dimension(S);
  V, VtoM, MtoV := VectorSpace(M);
  VS := VectorSpace(S); // subspace of V
  t1 := Cputime(t0);
  vprintf User2: "    order %o: dim S = %o (%o s)\n", n, dS, t1;
  if dS eq 0 then return <0, 2, PF!1, z>; end if;
  // The winding element and its cuspidal projection e'.
  e := MtoV(WindingElement(M));
  ops := AssociativeArray(Integers()); // sparse Hecke operators, indexed by l
  ops[2] := sparse_hecke(M, 2);
  e1 := eisenstein_killer(e, ops[2], 2, chi(2)); // a vector in V, not in VS (much faster)
  if IsZero(e1) then
    ops[3] := sparse_hecke(M, 3);
    e1 := eisenstein_killer(e, ops[3], 3, chi(3));
    // If e1 = 0 again, then (barring a coincidence modulo q)
    // the cuspidal projection of e vanishes, i.e., every newform of character chi
    // would be strange. This does not happen for p < 10^5.
    // The code below then reports a candidate with defect dim S.
  end if;
  t2 := Cputime(t0);
  // Common case: the Krylov space of e' under T_2 is all of S.
  rows := krylov_rows(e1, ops[2], dS);
  r := Rank(Matrix(rows));
  t3 := Cputime(t0);
  vprintf User2: "      T_2, e' (%o s); Krylov rank %o (%o s)\n", t2-t1, r, t3-t2;
  if r eq dS then
    vprintf User2: "      ==> not strange (total %o s)\n", t3;
    return <0, 2, PF!1, z>;
  end if;
  // Rank deficiency. Let W be the module generated by e' under the T_l
  // with l in gens; initially gens = [2], and W (the Krylov space) is T_2-stable
  // since the Krylov sequence has become linearly dependent.
  // We enlarge gens whenever W turns out not to be stable under some T_l
  // with l <= L_MAX, and, if necessary, until some T_l with l in gens
  // separates the quotient S/W from W. If W becomes all of S, chi is not strange.
  W := sub<VS | rows>;
  delete rows;
  gens := [2];
  lp := 0;
  kerpol := PF!1;
  while true do
    // stability under T_l for the primes l <= L_MAX not in gens
    for l in [l : l in PrimesUpTo(L_MAX) | l ne p and l notin gens] do
      T := sparse_hecke(M, l);
      if VS!(Random(W)*T) notin W then
        vprintf User2: "      Hecke module not stable under T_%o; enlarging\n", l;
        Append(~gens, l);
        ops[l] := T;
        W := saturate(W, VS, ops, gens, dS);
        if Dimension(W) eq dS then
          vprintf User2: "      ==> not strange (total %o s)\n", Cputime(t0);
          return <0, 2, PF!1, z>;
        end if;
      end if;
    end for;
    r := Dimension(W);
    d := dS - r;
    vprintf User2: "      Hecke module of e' under T_l, l in %o: dimension %o (%o s)\n",
                   Sort(gens), r, Cputime(t0)-t3;
    // The strange quotient Q = S/W (of dimension d) and the kernel polynomial:
    // the characteristic polynomial of T_l on Q for the smallest l in gens
    // such that kerpol(T_l) is injective on W.
    Q, pi := quo<VS | W>;
    assert Dimension(Q) eq d;
    B := [Q.i @@ pi : i in [1..d]];
    BW := Basis(W);
    found := false;
    for l in Sort(gens) do
      mat := Matrix([Eltseq(pi(VS!(b*ops[l]))) : b in B]);
      kerpol := CharacteristicPolynomial(mat);
      if r eq 0 or Rank(Matrix([pol_mat_vec(kerpol, ops[l], b) : b in BW])) eq r then
        found := true;
        lp := l;
        break;
      end if;
    end for;
    if found then break; end if;
    // No generator separates: add the next prime to gens and try again.
    l := 1;
    repeat l := NextPrime(l); until l ne p and l notin gens;
    error if l gt 200, "screen_character: no separating Hecke operator T_l with l <= 200";
    vprintf User2: "      no separating operator among T_l, l in %o; adding T_%o\n", Sort(gens), l;
    Append(~gens, l);
    ops[l] := sparse_hecke(M, l);
    W := saturate(W, VS, ops, gens, dS);
    if Dimension(W) eq dS then
      vprintf User2: "      ==> not strange (total %o s)\n", Cputime(t0);
      return <0, 2, PF!1, z>;
    end if;
  end while;
  vprintf User1: "    ==> order %o: candidate with defect %o\n", n, d;
  vprintf User2: "      kernel polynomial for T_%o found (total %o s)\n", lp, Cputime(t0);
  return <d, lp, kerpol, z>;
end function;

// Screen all even characters of prime modulus p (one for each order n > 1
// dividing (p-1)/2). Returns the sequence of candidates, each as a tuple
//   <p, n, q, z, d, l, coeffs>
// with q the auxiliary prime, z = chi(PrimitiveRoot(p)) as an integer,
// and coeffs the coefficients of kerpol as integers (see screen_character).
// The sequence is empty if and only if p is certified to be not strange.
function screen_prime(p)
  assert IsPrime(p);
  if p le 3 then return []; end if;
  t0 := Cputime();
  q := aux_prime(p);
  Fq := GF(q);
  G := DirichletGroup(p, Fq);
  assert #G eq p-1;
  cands := [];
  for n in [n : n in Divisors(ExactQuotient(p-1, 2)) | n gt 1] do
    chi := G.1^ExactQuotient(#G, n);
    assert Order(chi) eq n and IsEven(chi);
    d, l, kerpol, z := Explode(screen_character(chi));
    if d gt 0 then
      Append(~cands, <p, n, q, Integers()!z, d, l,
                      [Integers()!c : c in Coefficients(kerpol)]>);
    end if;
  end for;
  vprintf User1: "  p = %o: %o candidate(s) (%o s)\n", p, #cands, Cputime(t0);
  return cands;
end function;

//============================================================================
// Step 1': lean screening (Wiedemann / Berlekamp-Massey)
//============================================================================
//
// A variant of screen_character that certifies "chi is not strange" without
// dense linear algebra, for use beyond the range of Table 2 (see the header).
// It gives the same results (the candidate tuples are produced by the
// original code, to which it falls back); the code of record for Table 2 is
// screen_character above.

// The Hecke operator T_l as a sparse matrix; the dense matrix that Magma
// caches in M is discarded.
function sparse_hecke_lean(M, l)
  T := SparseMatrix(HeckeOperator(M, l));
  M`hecke_operator := [];
  return T;
end function;

// The linear complexity (Berlekamp-Massey) of the sequence u . (v T^i),
// i < 2 k, for a random vector u in V: a lower bound for dim F_q[T] v, with
// equality with probability at least 1 - k/q.
function krylov_dimension_bound(v, T, k, V, Fq)
  u := Random(V);
  uc := Matrix(Fq, Degree(V), 1, Eltseq(u));
  seq := [Fq| ];
  for i in [1..2*k] do
    Append(~seq, (v*uc)[1]);
    v := v*T;
  end for;
  C, L := BerlekampMassey(seq);
  return L;
end function;

// For a sequence s_0, ..., s_(K-1) over F_q of linear complexity L: the monic
// polynomial m of degree L and the polynomial N of degree < L such that the
// expansion of N/m in powers of 1/x begins with s_0/x + s_1/x^2 + ... (all K
// given terms). The assertions check exactly this property of the output of
// BerlekampMassey (C is the connection polynomial: the product of C and
// sum_k s_k x^k has no terms of degree L, ..., K-1).
function sequence_generator(seq, PF)
  K := #seq;
  C, L := BerlekampMassey(seq);
  C := PF!Coefficients(C);
  assert Coefficient(C, 0) eq 1;
  prod := PF!seq * C;
  assert forall{k : k in [L..K-1] | Coefficient(prod, k) eq 0};
  m := PF![Coefficient(C, L - i) : i in [0..L]];
  N := PF![Coefficient(prod, L - 1 - i) : i in [0..L-1]];
  return m, N;
end function;

// A certificate for dim (F_q[T] v1 + F_q[T] v2) = 2 d, where 2 d = k is the
// dimension of a T-stable space containing v1 and v2. With random vectors
// u1, u2, let S_i be the 2 x 2 matrix (v_a T^i . u_b), for i < K = k + 4.
// Each entry sequence is generated by some N_ab/m_ab (sequence_generator);
// let m be the lcm of the m_ab and P = (N_ab m/m_ab), so that P/m expands to
// S_0/x + S_1/x^2 + ... up to the term S_(K-1). We return true if
//   deg m = d,  m is squarefree,  and  gcd(m, det P) = 1.
// Claim: then the dimension is 2 d. Proof: over the algebraic closure,
// P/m = sum_lambda R_lambda/(x - lambda) with R_lambda = P(lambda)/m'(lambda)
// invertible for each of the d distinct roots lambda of m, so that
// S_i = sum_lambda lambda^i R_lambda for i < K. The block Hankel matrix
// H = (S_(i+j)), 0 <= i, j < d, (it involves S_i for i <= 2 d - 2 < K only)
// is therefore B^t D B with B the Kronecker product of the Vandermonde matrix
// of the roots with the 2 x 2 identity and D the block diagonal matrix of
// the R_lambda; both are invertible, so H has rank 2 d. On the other hand,
// H = X Y, where the rows of X are the vectors v_a T^i (i < d) and the
// columns of Y are the T^j u_b, so 2 d = rank H <= rank X
// <= dim (F_q[T] v1 + F_q[T] v2).
function two_generator_certificate(v1, v2, T, k, V, Fq)
  if IsOdd(k) then return false; end if;
  d := k div 2;
  PF := PolynomialRing(Fq);
  u1 := Matrix(Fq, Degree(V), 1, Eltseq(Random(V)));
  u2 := Matrix(Fq, Degree(V), 1, Eltseq(Random(V)));
  s11 := [Fq| ]; s12 := [Fq| ]; s21 := [Fq| ]; s22 := [Fq| ];
  for i in [1..k+4] do
    Append(~s11, (v1*u1)[1]); Append(~s12, (v1*u2)[1]);
    Append(~s21, (v2*u1)[1]); Append(~s22, (v2*u2)[1]);
    v1 := v1*T; v2 := v2*T;
  end for;
  m11, N11 := sequence_generator(s11, PF);
  m12, N12 := sequence_generator(s12, PF);
  m21, N21 := sequence_generator(s21, PF);
  m22, N22 := sequence_generator(s22, PF);
  m := Lcm([m11, m12, m21, m22]);
  if Degree(m) ne d or Gcd(m, Derivative(m)) ne 1 then return false; end if;
  detP := ((N11*(m div m11))*(N22*(m div m22))
           - (N12*(m div m12))*(N21*(m div m21))) mod m;
  return Gcd(m, detP) eq 1;
end function;

// Same input and output as screen_character.
function screen_character_lean(chi : trials := 4)
  t0 := Cputime();
  p := Modulus(chi);
  n := Order(chi);
  Fq := BaseRing(chi);
  PF := PolynomialRing(Fq);
  z := chi(PrimitiveRoot(p));
  M := ModularSymbols(chi, 2, +1);
  // dim S = dim M - 2: the boundary space is spanned by the cusps 0 and oo
  // (neither is killed by the even character chi), and the boundary map is
  // onto (it maps {0, oo} to [oo] - [0] and {0, a/b} to (chi(b)^-1 - 1)[0]).
  // The star involution fixes both cusps, so this holds for the plus
  // quotient as well.
  assert Rank(BoundaryMap(M)) eq 2;
  dS := Dimension(M) - 2;
  if dS le 0 then return <0, 2, PF!1, z>; end if;
  V, VtoM, MtoV := VectorSpace(M);
  e := MtoV(M!<1, [Cusps()| 0, Infinity()]>); // the winding element {0, oo}
  ops := AssociativeArray(Integers());
  ops[2] := sparse_hecke_lean(M, 2);
  e1 := eisenstein_killer(e, ops[2], 2, chi(2));
  // The quadratic character is special: a newform f and its complex conjugate
  // lie in the same space, and a_l(f) = chi(l) conj(a_l(f)) is real when
  // chi(l) = 1. An operator built from Hecke operators T_l with chi(l) = 1
  // therefore has every eigenvalue (at least) twice, and e' generates a
  // module of dimension at most dS/2 under it. When chi(2) = 1, we use a
  // second vector e'' = e' U, where U is a random linear combination of the
  // T_l for the primes l <= L_MAX with chi(l) = -1 (a_l(f) is then purely
  // imaginary; one such l need not suffice, as a_l(f) can vanish, e.g. for
  // forms with a small coefficient field), and certify that e' and e''
  // together generate S under T_2 alone; as e'' lies in the Hecke module of
  // e', that module is then S. No matrix of T_l is needed for this: in terms
  // of modular symbols,
  //   {0, oo} T_l = (1 + chi(l)) {0, oo} + sum_{0 < j < l} {j/l, oo},
  // and e'' is obtained from e U like e' from e, as U and T_2 commute.
  // If the certificate fails, the original code decides.
  if n eq 2 and chi(2) eq 1 and not IsZero(e1) then
    neg := [l : l in PrimesUpTo(L_MAX) | l ne p and chi(l) eq -1];
    if #neg eq 0 then
      l0 := NextPrime(L_MAX);
      while chi(l0) ne -1 do l0 := NextPrime(l0); end while;
      neg := [l0];
    end if;
    eU := V!0;
    for l in neg do
      eU +:= Random(Fq) * &+[MtoV(M!<1, [Cusps()| j/l, Infinity()]>) : j in [1..l-1]];
    end for;
    e2 := eisenstein_killer(eU, ops[2], 2, chi(2));
    t1 := Cputime(t0);
    ok := two_generator_certificate(e1, e2, ops[2], dS, V, Fq);
    vprintf User2: "    order 2: dim S = %o, two generators (e' and e' U, U from T_l, l in %o): %o (setup %o s, total %o s)\n",
                   dS, neg, ok select "certified" else "not certified", t1, Cputime(t0);
    if ok then return <0, 2, PF!1, z>; end if;
    vprintf User1: "    p = %o, order %o: not certified; using screen_character\n", p, n;
    return screen_character(chi);
  end if;
  if IsZero(e1) then
    ops[3] := sparse_hecke_lean(M, 3);
    e1 := eisenstein_killer(e, ops[3], 3, chi(3));
  end if;
  t1 := Cputime(t0);
  // Trial 1 uses T = T_2, trial k a random linear combination of T_2 and the
  // next k - 1 odd primes' Hecke operators. The span of e' under any element
  // T of the Hecke algebra is contained in the Hecke module of e', so linear
  // complexity dS certifies that the module is all of S (rigorously; a
  // smaller value can also be due to an unlucky choice, hence the trials).
  // Otherwise the original code decides (and produces the candidate data).
  lseq := [l : l in PrimesUpTo(L_MAX) | l gt 2 and l ne p];
  for trial in [1..Min(trials, #lseq + 1)] do
    if trial eq 1 then
      T := ops[2];
    else
      l := lseq[trial - 1];
      if not IsDefined(ops, l) then ops[l] := sparse_hecke_lean(M, l); end if;
      T := ops[2] + &+[Random(Fq)*ops[l] : l in lseq[1..trial-1]];
    end if;
    L := krylov_dimension_bound(e1, T, dS, V, Fq);
    vprintf User2: "    order %o: dim S = %o, linear complexity %o (trial %o; setup %o s, total %o s)\n",
                   n, dS, L, trial, t1, Cputime(t0);
    if L eq dS then return <0, 2, PF!1, z>; end if;
  end for;
  vprintf User1: "    p = %o, order %o: not certified; using screen_character\n", p, n;
  return screen_character(chi);
end function;

// Same as screen_prime, with the lean screening.
function screen_prime_lean(p)
  assert IsPrime(p);
  if p le 3 then return []; end if;
  t0 := Cputime();
  q := aux_prime(p);
  Fq := GF(q);
  G := DirichletGroup(p, Fq);
  cands := [];
  for n in [n : n in Divisors(ExactQuotient(p-1, 2)) | n gt 1] do
    chi := G.1^ExactQuotient(#G, n);
    d, l, kerpol, z := Explode(screen_character_lean(chi));
    if d gt 0 then
      Append(~cands, <p, n, q, Integers()!z, d, l,
                      [Integers()!c : c in Coefficients(kerpol)]>);
    end if;
  end for;
  vprintf User1: "  p = %o: %o candidate(s) (%o s)\n", p, #cands, Cputime(t0);
  return cands;
end function;

// Results files. screen_range and check_table2 can record their results
// in a file, one line per prime:
//   <p, [ candidates ]>,
// with the candidate tuples of screen_prime. When the same call is made again
// with the same file, the primes already recorded there are skipped,
// so that an interrupted run can simply be restarted.
// read_results(file) returns the recorded results as a list of tuples <p, candidates>.
function read_results(file)
  try
    s := Read(file);
  catch e
    return [* *];
  end try;
  if #s lt 2 then return [* *]; end if;
  return eval("[* " cat s[1..#s-2] cat " *]"); // strip the final ",\n"
end function;

procedure record_result(file, p, cands)
  if file ne "" then
    f := Open(file, "a");
    fprintf f, "%o,\n", <p, cands>;
    delete f;
  end if;
end procedure;

// The primes p with lo <= p <= hi that belong to the k-th of m parts (1 <= k <= m),
// part = <k, m> (every m-th prime in the interval, starting with the k-th),
// and are not yet recorded in the results file.
function primes_to_do(lo, hi, part, file)
  plist := PrimesInInterval(lo, hi);
  plist := [plist[i] : i in [part[1]..#plist by part[2]]];
  done := {Integers()| r[1] : r in read_results(file)};
  return [p : p in plist | p notin done];
end function;

// Screen all primes p with lo <= p <= hi (or the given part of them;
// see primes_to_do). Returns the sequence of the candidates found in this call.
function screen_range(lo, hi : part := <1, 1>, file := "")
  cands := [];
  for p in primes_to_do(lo, hi, part, file) do
    new := screen_prime(p);
    record_result(file, p, new);
    cands cat:= new;
  end for;
  return cands;
end function;

//============================================================================
// Step 2: verification in characteristic zero
//============================================================================

// pol: a monic polynomial over F_q whose roots are the reductions modulo q
//      of the eigenvalues a_l(f) of some newforms f (so |a_l(f)| <= 2 sqrt(l)
//      in every complex embedding) under the reduction map sigma: K -> F_q.
// Returns the lift of pol to K[x]: for each coefficient the element of O_K
// closest to 0 in the coset of elements reducing to it.
// The lifted coefficients are checked against the bound
//   B = Binomial(d, k) (2 sqrt(l))^k
// for the k-th elementary symmetric function of the roots.
//
// Correctness. Let m = [K:Q] and let Q be the prime ideal of O_K
// that is the kernel of sigma; it has norm q. If c and c' are elements
// of the same residue class modulo Q with |tau(c)|, |tau(c')| <= B
// for all complex embeddings tau, then x = c - c' lies in Q,
// so either x = 0 or |N_{K/Q}(x)| >= q;
// in the latter case the arithmetic-geometric mean inequality gives
// sum_tau |tau(x)|^2 >= m q^(2/m), whereas sum_tau |tau(x)|^2 <= 4 m B^2.
// Hence, if q > (2B)^m, each residue class contains at most one element
// satisfying the bound. The true coefficient satisfies the bound
// (the conjugates of a_l(f) are eigenvalues of the conjugate newforms
// and satisfy the Ramanujan bound), so the element found here
// is the true coefficient whenever q > (2B)^m, which is checked
// (with B enlarged by 0.01 to cover the floating point evaluation of the
// conjugates). The norm used by the closest vector search (the Euclidean
// norm of the coordinates with respect to the LLL-reduced basis of O_K,
// not the T_2 norm) only affects whether the small element is found, not
// its correctness.
function lift_polynomial(pol, sigma, l)
  K := Domain(sigma);
  Fq := Codomain(sigma);
  q := #Fq;
  OK := LLL(Integers(K));
  bas := Basis(OK);
  m := Degree(K);
  img := Matrix(Integers(), m, 1, [Integers()!sigma(K!b) : b in bas]);
  // lattice of coordinate vectors (w.r.t. bas) of elements of O_K reducing to 0
  L := Lattice(q*IdentityMatrix(Integers(), m)) + Lattice(KernelMatrix(img));
  d := Degree(pol);
  coeffs := [K| ];
  for i := 0 to d-1 do
    a := Coefficient(pol, i);
    sol := ChangeRing(Solution(ChangeRing(img, Fq), Vector([a])), Integers());
    clv := ClosestVectors(L, sol);
    error if #clv ne 1,
      "lift_polynomial: closest vector is not unique; increase Q_MIN";
    v := sol - clv[1];
    c := K!&+[v[j]*bas[j] : j in [1..m]];
    bound := Binomial(d, d-i) * (2*Sqrt(l))^(d-i);
    error if q lt (2*bound + 0.02)^m,
      "lift_polynomial: q is too small to determine the lift uniquely; increase Q_MIN";
    error if exists{cc : cc in Conjugates(c) | Abs(cc) gt bound + 0.01},
      "lift_polynomial: lift is too large to be correct; increase Q_MIN";
    Append(~coeffs, c);
  end for;
  Append(~coeffs, K!1);
  polK := PolynomialRing(K)!coeffs;
  assert forall{i : i in [0..d] | sigma(Coefficient(polK, i)) eq Coefficient(pol, i)};
  return polK;
end function;

// cand: a candidate <p, n, q, z, d, l, coeffs> as returned by screen_prime.
// Returns <n, strdim, orbits, confirmed>, where
// + strdim is the number of newforms with character in the Galois orbit of chi
//   that were proved to be strange,
// + orbits is the sequence of the sizes of their Galois orbits over Q, and
// + confirmed is true if and only if these account for the full defect d, i.e.,
//   there are no other strange newforms with character (conjugate to) chi.
//   (confirmed = false means that the deficiency found modulo q was spurious
//   or that q was unlucky; rerun with a larger L_MAX or a different Q_MIN.)
function verify_candidate(cand)
  t0 := Cputime();
  p, n, q, z, d, l, coeffs := Explode(cand);
  Fq := GF(q);
  z := Fq!z;
  pol := PolynomialRing(Fq)!coeffs;
  assert Degree(pol) eq d;
  // The character over K = Q(zeta_n) that reduces to the screening character
  // under sigma: zeta_n |-> z.
  K<zeta> := CyclotomicField(n);
  g := PrimitiveRoot(p);
  G := DirichletGroup(p, K);
  chi := G.1^ExactQuotient(#G, n);
  w := chi(g);
  j := [j : j in [1..n] | zeta^j eq w][1];
  chi := chi^Modinv(j, n);
  assert Order(chi) eq n and chi(g) eq zeta;
  sigma := hom<K -> Fq | z>;
  kerpol := lift_polynomial(pol, sigma, l);
  vprintf User2: "    p = %o, n = %o: kernel polynomial for T_%o is %o\n", p, n, l, kerpol;
  // The kernel of kerpol(T_l) on M; it is contained in the cuspidal subspace.
  M := ModularSymbols(chi, 2, +1);
  N := Kernel([<l, kerpol>], M);
  vprintf User2: "      dim M = %o, kernel of kerpol(T_%o) has dimension %o (%o s)\n",
                    Dimension(M), l, Dimension(N), Cputime(t0);
  if Dimension(N) ne d then
    printf "verify_candidate(p = %o, n = %o): kernel has dimension %o, expected %o;\n"
           cat " the candidate is not confirmed (spurious deficiency mod q = %o?)\n",
           p, n, Dimension(N), d, q;
    return <n, 0, [Integers()| ], false>;
  end if;
  assert IsCuspidal(N);
  dec := d eq 1 select [N] else NewformDecomposition(N);
  strange := [Integers()| Dimension(A) : A in dec | IsZero(WindingElementProjection(A))];
  phi := EulerPhi(n);
  confirmed := &+strange eq d;
  vprintf User1: "    p = %o, n = %o: pieces of dimensions %o over K, strange: %o (%o s)\n",
                    p, n, [Dimension(A) : A in dec], strange, Cputime(t0);
  if not confirmed then
    printf "verify_candidate(p = %o, n = %o): only %o of the %o dimensions of the kernel\n"
           cat " are strange; the candidate is not confirmed (spurious deficiency mod q = %o?)\n",
           p, n, &+strange, d, q;
  end if;
  return <n, phi*&+strange, [phi*s : s in strange], confirmed>;
end function;

//============================================================================
// Drivers
//============================================================================

// The strange characters of prime modulus p: returns a sequence of tuples
//   <n, strdim, orbits, confirmed>
// (one for each strange order n; see verify_candidate),
// which is empty if and only if p is not strange.
function strange_orders(p)
  return [verify_candidate(c) : c in screen_prime(p)];
end function;

// Same as strange_orders, with the lean screening.
function strange_orders_lean(p)
  return [verify_candidate(c) : c in screen_prime_lean(p)];
end function;

// Compare the verified results for the prime p with Table 2 and report;
// verified is the output of strange_orders(p)
// (or of verify_candidate on the candidates for p).
function agrees_with_table2(p, verified)
  result := [<r[1], r[2]> : r in verified | r[4]];
  expected := [<e[2], e[3]> : e in TABLE2 | e[1] eq p];
  if result ne expected then
    printf "MISMATCH for p = %o: found %o, Table 2 says %o\n", p, result, expected;
    return false;
  elif not IsEmpty(result) then
    printf "p = %o: %o, as in Table 2\n", p, result;
  end if;
  return true;
end function;

// Screen and verify all primes in [lo, hi] and compare with Table 2
// (an assertion fails at the end if there was a mismatch).
// To split the work over several Magma processes, run the k-th of m processes
// with
//   check_table2(lo, hi : part := <k, m>, file := "results_k.txt");
// it then handles every m-th prime in the interval, starting with the k-th,
// and records the screening results in the file, so that it can be
// restarted after an interruption (see read_results).
procedure check_table2(lo, hi : part := <1, 1>, file := "")
  t0 := Cputime();
  ok := true;
  for p in primes_to_do(lo, hi, part, file) do
    cands := screen_prime(p);
    record_result(file, p, cands);
    // (call first: "ok and:= ..." would skip the call once ok is false)
    ok := agrees_with_table2(p, [verify_candidate(c) : c in cands]) and ok;
  end for;
  printf "check_table2(%o, %o : part := %o): %o (%o s)\n", lo, hi, part,
         ok select "all results agree with Table 2" else "THERE WERE MISMATCHES", Cputime(t0);
  assert ok;
end procedure;

// Check the primes listed in Table 2 (or those in the given sequence).
procedure check_table2_primes(: primes := Sort(Setseq({e[1] : e in TABLE2})), file := "")
  for p in primes do
    check_table2(p, p : file := file);
  end for;
end procedure;

// Verify the candidates recorded in a results file (or several)
// and compare with Table 2; also reports the primes covered.
procedure check_results(files)
  if Type(files) eq MonStgElt then files := [files]; end if;
  ok := true;
  count := 0;
  for file in files do
    for r in read_results(file) do
      count +:= 1;
      ok := agrees_with_table2(r[1], [verify_candidate(c) : c in r[2]]) and ok;
    end for;
  end for;
  printf "check_results: %o primes, %o\n", count,
         ok select "all results agree with Table 2" else "THERE WERE MISMATCHES";
  assert ok;
end procedure;
